#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078259 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e4cc25e-b0e9-5935-86f7-c450c990cef8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Inverse Trigonometric Ratio"}] :question/id #uuid "200cec1b-b266-557f-a2ef-db02cf6d941a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3251" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\arcsin (0.2)$ in degrees to the nearest degree." :question/worked-solution "Using a calculator, we find \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0.2) & =(11.536...)^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{12}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078260 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078396 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81772ee2-6b04-559c-85a3-976fd12bbd74" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Unknown Angles Using Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "01ba80ea-e3d7-5372-9732-d721f039bae8" :question/math-academy-id "e-3252" :question/problem "If $\\cos\\theta=0.64$ and $\\tan\\alpha=1.25$, where $\\theta$ and $\\alpha$ are acute angles measured in degrees, find $\\theta+\\alpha$ in degrees to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\theta$ and $\\alpha$ we use $\\arccos$ and $\\arctan$ respectively. We get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\cos\\theta \t\t\t& = 0.64\\\n\\arccos(\\cos\\theta)\t& = \\arccos(0.64) \\\n\\theta&=\\arccos(0.64)\n}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\tan\\alpha \t\t\t& = 1.25 \\\n\\arctan(\\tan\\alpha)\t\t& =\\arctan(1.25) \\\n\\alpha&=\\arctan(1.25).\n}\n$$\n\nThen, using a calculator, we find\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\theta + \\alpha\t\t\t\t\t& =\\arccos(0.64)+\\arctan(1.25)\\\n&=(50.208...)^{\\circ} +  (51.340...)^{\\circ} \\\n\t& = (101.548...)^{\\circ}\t\\\n\t& \\approx 102^{\\circ}\n}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\n**Watch out!** If we round $\\theta$ and $\\alpha$ to the nearest integer, we get $\\theta \\approx 50^\\circ$ and $\\alpha \\approx 51^\\circ.$ After we sum  up the angles we get an incorrect result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta +\\alpha  & ≈{50}^{∘}+{51}^{∘}={101}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe correct answer is $102^\\circ,$ not $101^\\circ.$ This is a rounding error! To avoid this kind of error we have to round *after* we take the sum, not before."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078261 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8ccd79e-56a6-58fc-917c-8dc660ad2425" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Measure of an Angle Given the Lengths of Two Sides of a Right Triangle"}] :question/id #uuid "7a4aa28f-8a0b-556f-b6ce-beb674cd0234" :question/math-academy-id "e-3253" :question/problem "In a right triangle $\\triangle ABC,$ we have $AC = 9,$ $BC=13,$ and $m\\angle A = \\alpha.$ Given that $\\overline{AB}$ is the hypotenuse of the triangle, find the value of $\\alpha$ (rounded to one decimal place)." :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the triangle that's described.\n\n \n\n![](images/29/29ab71a468b9a57842e07ef74b8d8d770c7ebfacabb3735694c0dd40303ad220.png)\n\nSince we are given the lengths of the adjacent and opposite sides, we can use tangent to find the angle measurement. We have\n\n$$\n\\tan \\alpha=  \\dfrac{\\textrm{opposite}}{\\textrm{adjacent}}  = \\dfrac{13}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe now calculate $\\alpha$ using the inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\tan \\alpha &= \\dfrac{13}{9}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\arctan( \\tan \\alpha) &= \\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{13}{9} \\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\alpha &= \\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{13}{9} \\right)\\[5pt]\n&=(55.304\\,8...)^\\circ\\[5pt]\t\n\t\t &\\approx 55.3^\\circ\n\t}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e4cc25e-b0e9-5935-86f7-c450c990cef8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Inverse Trigonometric Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12e5b434-b339-517f-8e76-65e3a8e74c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{63}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520805} {:answer/id #uuid "392938b3-fafd-5d04-96a2-6f65b79f2214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{59}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520889} {:answer/id #uuid "5294be99-f335-5f00-9dc1-33495c1ddccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{56}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520951} {:answer/id #uuid "592dd33b-32ce-5762-b91a-04fda1691cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{49}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520968} {:answer/id #uuid "936b2fb6-abb5-5942-9464-f53912a834de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{67}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5294be99-f335-5f00-9dc1-33495c1ddccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{56}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520951} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e7bf571-3da0-5ba9-9c54-e3a915fafd86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d8ea2fc-3731-5b48-8288-39aacc74f9f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-122419" :question/problem "Find the value of  $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})$ in degrees to the nearest degree." :question/worked-solution "Using a calculator, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) & =(56.309…)^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{56}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest degree."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078396 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81772ee2-6b04-559c-85a3-976fd12bbd74" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Unknown Angles Using Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "170b7b59-9320-58fb-b101-0feb53b6825e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "101.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "170b7b59-9320-58fb-b101-0feb53b6825e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "101.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520816} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e6971a9-86cf-535c-854c-c7408df6402f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d28482b-a0c8-53e6-a980-b5acf56e83ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-241885" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =0.64$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha =1.25$, where $\\theta $ and $\\alpha $ are acute angles measured in degrees, then $\\theta +\\alpha $ in degrees to one decimal place is{{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\theta $ and $\\alpha $ we use $arccos$ and $arctan$ respectively. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0.64 \\\\ \\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.64) \\\\ \\theta  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.64)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =1.25 \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha ) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1.25) \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1.25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using a calculator, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta +\\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.64)+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1.25) \\\\  & =(50.208\\,1…)^{∘}+(51.340\\,1…)^{∘} \\\\  & =(101.548\\,2…)^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{101.5}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078396 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81772ee2-6b04-559c-85a3-976fd12bbd74" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Unknown Angles Using Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26830c29-2764-58cb-a64e-6ccf68e9b5b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{15.2}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520849} {:answer/id #uuid "8f2f93c0-6c57-56f3-a190-9a0cdd301723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{13.2}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521087} {:answer/id #uuid "92def250-4bc5-5dc3-912c-b81178106d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{14.6}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521096} {:answer/id #uuid "bb24e06f-b9b1-5d89-ba4f-db1817a41a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{14.0}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521181} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc5c902-1cf7-59f6-aff0-0fc57a3b65db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{17.0}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb24e06f-b9b1-5d89-ba4f-db1817a41a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{14.0}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521181} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ff543ef-f7c1-5af4-b11c-da6db40af722" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9e6b263-d73b-5513-9574-32caadf21e21" :question/math-academy-id "q-27912" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =0.25$ and $\\theta $ is acute, then the value of $\\theta $ in degrees to one decimal place is" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\theta $ we use $arctan.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =0.25 \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0.25) \\\\ \\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0.25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0.25). \\\\  & =(14.036\\,2…)^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{14.0}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8ccd79e-56a6-58fc-917c-8dc660ad2425" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Measure of an Angle Given the Lengths of Two Sides of a Right Triangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12483b7c-75aa-5d6b-b9c2-3a38b6a59e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{32.8}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520803} {:answer/id #uuid "351eb816-1272-52d2-90b9-bfc766cd81a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{24.6}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520875} {:answer/id #uuid "5d891148-a3a7-5819-a73c-eeda3eab70c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{27.5}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520974} {:answer/id #uuid "d1176aad-386c-5cb7-8300-8d79fa09268e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30.5}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521233} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b32859-f535-52b8-b810-054604e1f38b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30.2}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d891148-a3a7-5819-a73c-eeda3eab70c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{27.5}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520974} :answer-field/id #uuid "03c2a9e1-24c8-5afe-a713-5a7daad4ef0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63ad50c7-f590-525f-9856-7ab603587d0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-4546" :question/problem "![](images/40/404e9d30d45485251a19cdb2ccfef4ea53509a66437c99908eda065ad99e5e82.png)\n\nRounded to one decimal place, $x≈$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given the lengths of the opposite side and the hypotenuse, we can use sine to find the angle measurement. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{6}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now calculate $x$ using inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{6}{13} \\\\ \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{6}{13}) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{6}{13}) \\\\ x & =(27.486\\,4…)^{∘} \\\\ x & ≈{27.5}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8ccd79e-56a6-58fc-917c-8dc660ad2425" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Measure of an Angle Given the Lengths of Two Sides of a Right Triangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6081e871-d742-538d-8c66-3f9650b689ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{68.3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520983} {:answer/id #uuid "74962891-b2f9-59ae-a10f-151fcb5564ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{59.1}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521023} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0335af-9d97-523b-9116-2d597fe88f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{37.4}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521225} {:answer/id #uuid "d954596e-a926-5218-a588-5616b9c7a0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{88.1}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521249} {:answer/id #uuid "ee4c62da-63ab-593f-8dfe-728e7ea4a176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{46.3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6081e871-d742-538d-8c66-3f9650b689ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{68.3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520983} :answer-field/id #uuid "183e2a0a-fbdb-53c6-baa6-7dc46eb5ab7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9231519-804c-5398-8af6-73a4e2d373bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-4743" :question/problem "![](images/61/61dc032461e469146f11eacac4f917e86d40b3078dc2d33ac89e8e0b31eefd48.png)\n\nRounded to one decimal place, $x≈$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given the lengths of the adjacent side and the hypotenuse, we can use cosine to find the angle measurement. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{10}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now calculate $x$ using inverse cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{10}{27} \\\\ \\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{10}{27}) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{10}{27}) \\\\ x & =(68.261\\,5…)^{∘} \\\\ x & ≈{68.3}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e4cc25e-b0e9-5935-86f7-c450c990cef8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Inverse Trigonometric Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a6e61cd-d186-5792-84d0-8d7d67600c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{68}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520780} {:answer/id #uuid "2a8bc0df-5aa9-5ce3-bcbf-a8928812b638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{66}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520854} {:answer/id #uuid "9bfcee41-0fad-566f-9af6-d545060b165d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{71}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521117} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e2bbae-35c1-5d0c-a925-bfeae261bad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{59}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521205} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e3e47c-bd55-5a69-9a47-9986d09220c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{63}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a6e61cd-d186-5792-84d0-8d7d67600c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{68}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520780} :answer-field/id #uuid "86a8665b-bde3-52c7-b1d3-5fddd8560e4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07caf841-c23b-5d01-856b-51dc74025403" :question/math-academy-id "q-76576" :question/problem "Using a calculator, find the value of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})$ in degrees to the nearest degree." :question/worked-solution "Using a calculator, we find \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) & =(41.810…)^{∘}+(26.565…)^{∘} \\\\  & =(68.375…)^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{68}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest degree."}]}
